Note `commit()` on failure reloads state from disk — so `r.state` gets replaced with the on-disk snapshot, which removes the in-memory `r.state.Requests[key]` entry. Wait — commit reloads only if `load` succeeds. In the failure injection, the original registry.json is intact, so load succeeds and r.state is replaced with the pre-rejection state. So the request record is gone from memory too. Good.

But currently the code does `_ = r.commit()` and returns the business error. The fix:

```go
